What not to do

Here is where people sabotage themselves.

Do not:
build 8 microservices
use every tool on Earth
spend 3 days picking the “best” language
make frontend the focus
manually create resources in AWS console
keep secrets in .env committed to git
skip monitoring
skip failure testing
skip documentation
claim “production-grade” without proving recovery and observability

That last one is the biggest self-deception.

Cost control

You are not a company. Do not act like one with unlimited budget.

Keep costs sane
use small node group
destroy infra when not using it
use dev-sized RDS
avoid unnecessary NAT data transfer
clean ECR old images
use one environment at a time if needed
maybe use t-class where possible if supported
turn off Grafana/monitoring lab only when needed if separate

Also document:

what would change for real production scale
what you kept smaller for budget reasons

That makes you look mature, not cheap.

How to present this in interviews

Use this structure:

1. Problem

“I wanted to simulate a realistic cloud-native platform with synchronous API handling, asynchronous background processing, durable state, observability, CI/CD, and resilience.”

2. Architecture

Explain flow in 60 seconds.

3. Why these tools
Terraform for reproducibility
EKS for orchestration/platform depth
RDS for managed relational durability
SQS for decoupling and retry behavior
Redis for fast ephemeral state
GitHub Actions for automated delivery
Prometheus/Grafana/Loki for observability
4. Operational thinking

“I added health probes, HPA, DLQ, resource constraints, dashboards, and runbooks.”

5. Failure handling

“I tested pod failure, queue backlog, rollback, and DB failover impact.”

6. Lessons

Talk about tradeoffs, not only success.

That makes you sound like an engineer, not a course graduate.

